WEBVTT

cue-VID_U4_04-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Frostvale Foods is choosing a site for a new frozen-food plant. Two pins glow on the map: one near the port, one near the capital city. The finance team must decide where to build. Watch the map zoom, the two pins pulse, and six factor icons, including government incentives, orbit each location. The decision will mix hard numbers with softer judgements.

cue-VID_U4_04-2
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Quantitative factors can be measured in money. Labour costs, transport to market, raw material delivery, utilities and government incentives all have price tags. At the port site, labour is 300,000 dollars, transport 80,000, materials 40,000 and utilities 60,000, with a 30,000 dollar incentive. Watch each cost label drop into a column and a total counter begin adding them up before any decision is made.

cue-VID_U4_04-3
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Qualitative factors are harder to price. Quality of life affects whether skilled engineers want to move. Local labour skills affect training time. Environmental impact affects the plant's reputation and licence to operate. Watch three translucent panels fade in beside the cost column: a lifestyle icon, a skills badge and a green river. These factors cannot be ignored simply because they lack a price tag.

cue-VID_U4_04-4
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Let's total the port site, called Site A, using annual costs. Labour 300,000 dollars plus transport 80,000 gives 380,000. Add raw material delivery of 40,000 to reach 420,000. Add utilities of 60,000 to reach 480,000. Now subtract the government incentive of 30,000, leaving 450,000 dollars a year. Watch five cost tiles stack in the column, with the green incentive tile flipping the total down to 450,000. That is the number to beat.

cue-VID_U4_04-5
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Now let us total the city site, called Site B, carefully. Labour 360,000, transport 20,000, materials 70,000 and utilities 50,000, with no incentive. Add them: 360,000 plus 20,000 is 380,000; plus 70,000 is 450,000; plus 50,000 gives 500,000 dollars. Site A is 50,000 dollars cheaper. Watch the second column rise to 500,000, with a 50,000 dollar saving badge on Site A based on today's demand.

cue-VID_U4_04-6
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Now demand shifts toward the capital city. Serving that market from the port becomes costlier: Site A's transport rises from 80,000 to 140,000 dollars, an increase of 60,000. Recalculate Site A: 300,000 plus 140,000 plus 40,000 plus 60,000 minus 30,000 equals 510,000 dollars. Site B stays at 500,000, so it is now 10,000 dollars cheaper. Watch the transport tile grow and the totals swap places as customers move closer to the city.

cue-VID_U4_04-7
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:20.000
The map tells the same story. As buyers cluster in the capital, the port pin dims and the city pin brightens. Because transport cost rises with distance, the landed cost of frozen meals rises; therefore the cheaper site changes; so Frostvale should shift its plant toward the city. Watch a delivery truck retrace a longer route, then the chosen pin slide across the map.

cue-VID_U4_04-8
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Should Frostvale choose purely on cost? After the demand shift, the city site saves 10,000 dollars a year. Yet the port may offer skilled workers and cheaper land, while the city brings higher wages and tighter rules. Watch a split screen: a green saving badge on one side, amber quality of life and environment icons on the other. So cost guides the choice, but it does not settle it alone.

cue-VID_U4_04-9
00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Apply the idea. If Site A's transport rose to 160,000 dollars instead of 140,000, by how much would its total exceed Site B's? Add 20,000 to 510,000 for 530,000. That is 30,000 above 500,000. Watch the transport tile stretch and the cost gap widen on screen.

